<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Ancient Veneti</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 49 BCE (approx.) · capital Ateste</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Iron Age people of north-eastern Italy, centred on Ateste and Patavium, famed for horse-breeding and bronze work, who became Rome's peaceful allies and gave their name to Venice.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/veneti">Veneti</a> emerged in the plain between the Po, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in the early 1st millennium BCE, forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/este-veneto">Este</a> culture from about 1000 BCE. Greek and Roman writers linked them to the Eneti of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-paphlagonia">Paphlagonia</a> or to Trojan refugees under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antenor">Antenor</a>, legends <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-padua">Padua</a> later embraced, but archaeology shows a mainly local development.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their main centres were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/este-veneto">Ateste</a> (Este), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Patavium</a> (<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-padua">Padua</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicetia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altinum">Altinum</a>, each probably self-governing. They wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetic">Venetic</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European</a> language, in an alphabet adapted from Etruscan, known from hundreds of votive inscriptions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> situlae decorated with processions and banquets, amber trade from the Baltic, and celebrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse-breeding</a> marked their culture; the healing goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reitia">Reitia</a> had a major sanctuary at Este.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gauls</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/veneti">Veneti</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the 3rd century BCE and were absorbed gradually and without conquest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetic">Venetic</a>, and Caesar's grant of citizenship in 49 BCE completed integration. Their name lived on in the Roman region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> et <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/histria">Histria</a> and, through it, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ancient-veneti.jpg" alt="Ancient Veneti" width="320" height="389"><figcaption>Image: Iron_Age_Italy.png: User:Dbachmann derivative work: Ewan ar born translator: Manlleus (ca) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iron_Age_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 49 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ateste</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Venetic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Venetic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Italy</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Veneti, Venetic people</dd></dl></aside></div>
